s required."},{"variantId":"15500000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","dimension":"failureConsequence","proposedScore":58,"anchorTier":60,"rationale":"Candidate anchor generated from the exact Submaximal Linear Multi-Step Deceleration to Bilateral Stick task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."},{"variantId":"15500000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","dimension":"impact","proposedScore":52,"anchorTier":60,"rationale":"Candidate anchor generated from the exact Submaximal Linear Multi-Step Deceleration to Bilateral Stick task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."},{"variantId":"15500000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","dimension":"workCapacityDemand","proposedScore":28,"anchorTier":20,"rationale":"Candidate anchor generated from the exact Submaximal Linear Multi-Step Deceleration to Bilateral Stick task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."}],"evidence":[{"sectionKey":"identity","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/contentassets/116c55d64e1343d2b264e05aaf158a91/basics_of_strength_and_conditioning_manual.pdf","sourceTitle":"Basics of Strength and Conditioning Manual","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":84,"claims":["Landing-control exercises must distinguish a non-jump drop, a jump or hop with flight, an elevated drop, and a run-to-stop because approach momentum and drop height change the task.","Submaximal Linear Deceleration to Stick must encode takeoff, landing laterality, direction, approach speed, external height, rebound intent, and required hold as identity, variant, or dosage dimensions."]},{"sectionKey":"taxonomy","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9474351/","sourceTitle":"Biomechanical and Neuromuscular Performance Requirements of Horizontal Deceleration: A Review with Implications for Random Intermittent Multi-Directional Sports","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine - Open","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":90,"claims":["Landing and horizontal deceleration both train force attenuation, but horizontal stopping additionally requires braking impulse to reduce whole-body momentum.","Submaximal Linear Deceleration to Stick belongs to landing and braking control; reactive rebounding, maximal jumping, change of direction, and conditioning are separate programming intents."]},{"sectionKey":"anatomy","sourceUrl":"https://worldathletics.org/download/downloadnsa?filename=8c33cc0b-ba23-4d3d-9dbe-168e10d5fcfb.pdf&urlslug=plyometrics-for-beginners-basic-considerati","sourceTitle":"Plyometrics for Beginners: Basic Considerations","sourcePublisher":"World Athletics","sourceKind":"governing_body","evidenceQuality":76,"claims":["Force absorption is shared across the foot and ankle, knee, and hip rather than isolated to one joint.","The card should identify plantar flexors and intrinsic foot muscles, quadriceps, hamstrings, gluteals, hip abductors and external rotators, and trunk stabilizers with task-specific primary and secondary roles."]},{"sectionKey":"biomechanics","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9474351/","sourceTitle":"Biomechanical and Neuromuscular Performance Requirements of Horizontal Deceleration: A Review with Implications for Random Intermittent Multi-Directional Sports","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine - Open","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":90,"claims":["Effective braking coordinates lower-limb flexion, centre-of-mass control, foot placement, and force direction to attenuate and apply braking force.","Observable quality includes quiet controlled contact, whole-foot pressure, coordinated ankle-knee-hip flexion, knee tracking, stable pelvis and trunk, and ownership of the final position without extra steps."]},{"sectionKey":"difficulty","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/contentassets/116c55d64e1343d2b264e05aaf158a91/basics_of_strength_and_conditioning_manual.pdf","sourceTitle":"Basics of Strength and Conditioning Manual","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":84,"claims":["Plyometric and landing intensity changes with speed, external height, body mass, external load, and task complexity.","Exercise difficulty must score technical complexity and physical demand directly, including coordination, impact, supervision, work capacity, and consequence of error; it must not use a skill-library level."]},{"sectionKey":"load_fatigue_recovery","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9474351/","sourceTitle":"Biomechanical and Neuromuscular Performance Requirements of Horizontal Deceleration: A Review with Implications for Random Intermittent Multi-Directional Sports","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine - Open","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":90,"claims":["Horizontal deceleration creates high impact peaks, loading rates, and eccentric muscle demand; rapid or repeated exposures can contribute to muscle damage and need volume management.","Submaximal Linear Deceleration to Stick needs contact count, approach speed or drop height, laterality, eccentric braking demand, joint and tissue stress, performance-loss criteria, and recovery guidance rather than repetition count alone."]},{"sectionKey":"constraints","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10254820/","sourceTitle":"Training interventions to reduce the risk of injury to the lower extremity joints during landing movements in adult athletes: a systematic review and meta-analysis","sourcePublisher":"BMJ Open Sport & Exercise Medicine","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":89,"claims":["Landing interventions depend on appropriate materials, coaching knowledge, usable space, and task-specific progression.","The delivery profile should require a level non-slip surface, clear landing and run-out zones, stable equipment where applicable, suitable footwear, spacing, coach sightline, and a population-specific readiness screen."]},{"sectionKey":"dosage","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/contentassets/116c55d64e1343d2b264e05aaf158a91/basics_of_strength_and_conditioning_manual.pdf","sourceTitle":"Basics of Strength and Conditioning Manual","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":84,"claims":["Landing quality and force absorption should be learned before higher-speed, higher-height, repeated, or reactive plyometric work.","Prescribe low-repetition quality sets, landing contacts per side, approach speed or height, hold duration, rest, cue complexity, and progression criteria; stop before fatigue changes mechanics."]},{"sectionKey":"instructions","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6208302/","sourceTitle":"The Use of Augmented Information for Reducing Anterior Cruciate Ligament Injury Risk During Jump Landings: A Systematic Review","sourcePublisher":"Journal of Athletic Training","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":87,"claims":["Prescriptive instructions and feedback can improve landing mechanics, while excessive or poorly targeted information can interfere with learning.","Use a small cue set: land quietly, share force through ankle-knee-hip flexion, keep knee over the middle toes, control pelvis and trunk, and hold the finish without a touch-down or extra step."]},{"sectionKey":"safety_stop_rules","sourceUrl":"https://worldathletics.org/download/downloadnsa?filename=8c33cc0b-ba23-4d3d-9dbe-168e10d5fcfb.pdf&urlslug=plyometrics-for-beginners-basic-considerati","sourceTitle":"Plyometrics for Beginners: Basic Considerations","sourcePublisher":"World Athletics","sourceKind":"governing_body","evidenceQuality":76,"claims":["Loud contact, poor stabilization, loss of alignment, or inability to use the ankle, knee, and hip together indicate that intensity or volume exceeds current control.","Stop for pain, giving way, dizziness, repeated valgus or trunk collapse, uncontrolled foot contact, extra steps, loss of the required hold, unsafe surface or spacing, or persistent left-right asymmetry."]},{"sectionKey":"programming","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6592422/","sourceTitle":"Evidence-Based Best-Practice Guidelines for Preventing Anterior Cruciate Ligament Injuries in Young Female Athletes: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis","sourcePublisher":"The American Journal of Sports Medicine","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":91,"claims":["Landing stabilization and lower-body strength are recurring components of effective neuromuscular injury-prevention programs.","Submaximal Linear Deceleration to Stick belongs after general preparation and before high-intensity jumping, sprinting, or change-of-direction work; rehabilitation and return-to-sport assessment require separate clinical context."]},{"sectionKey":"athlete_support","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6208302/","sourceTitle":"The Use of Augmented Information for Reducing Anterior Cruciate Ligament Injury Risk During Jump Landings: A Systematic Review","sourcePublisher":"Journal of Athletic Training","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":87,"claims":["Athletes benefit from specific feedback tied to observable landing actions rather than abstract risk language.","Athlete support should show the start and finish positions, state the landing target and hold time, define a successful repetition, provide one primary cue, and explain the stop signal."]},{"sectionKey":"coach_support","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10254820/","sourceTitle":"Training interventions to reduce the risk of injury to the lower extremity joints during landing movements in adult athletes: a systematic review and meta-analysis","sourcePublisher":"BMJ Open Sport & Exercise Medicine","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":89,"claims":["Coach education and practical implementation affect whether landing interventions can be delivered effectively.","Coach support should provide front and side observation points, pass-fail criteria, contact counting, left-right comparison, regressions, progressions, equipment checks, and rules for reducing speed, height, distance, or laterality."]},{"sectionKey":"accessibility","sourceUrl":"https://worldathletics.org/download/downloadnsa?filename=8c33cc0b-ba23-4d3d-9dbe-168e10d5fcfb.pdf&urlslug=plyometrics-for-beginners-basic-considerati","sourceTitle":"Plyometrics for Beginners: Basic Considerations","sourcePublisher":"World Athletics","sourceKind":"governing_body","evidenceQuality":76,"claims":["Beginners should establish landing and stabilization control before progressing from bilateral to unilateral, longer, faster, or shock-loading tasks.","Accessibility can be improved with visual floor targets, demonstrated start and finish shapes, plain-language cues, reduced amplitude, external support, longer holds, and reduced sensory or decision load without assigning an exercise skill level."]},{"sectionKey":"alternates","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/contentassets/116c55d64e1343d2b264e05aaf158a91/basics_of_strength_and_conditioning_manual.pdf","sourceTitle":"Basics of Strength and Conditioning Manual","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":84,"claims":["In-place landings, jumps covering distance, repeated hops, and elevated or externally loaded tasks have different intensity and programming implications.","Direction, laterality, support, cueing, and low amplitude are usually variants; rebound intent, approach momentum, external height, or a change to testing or return-to-sport purpose may require a distinct definition or contextual profile."]},{"sectionKey":"media","sourceUrl":"https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/171780?expand=PrivacyEnhancedMode&hl=en","sourceTitle":"Embed videos and playlists","sourcePublishe